# `speechSynthesis.getVoices()` is a catalogue the operating system installs, so it names the platform
# and the locale in one read. Measured on the shipping engine's `persona` capture: three
# `Microsoft Huihui / Kangkang / Yaoyao - Chinese (Simplified, PRC)` voices, every one of them
# `zh-CN`, on a machine that claims `en-US` and an Apple M3. Two of assertion 6's rules score exactly
# that -- `voice-catalogue-belongs-to-the-claimed-os` and `voice-catalogue-covers-the-claimed-locale`
# -- and they were the last two floors left on the `persona` case after T05/T06 (6.11).
#
# THE SHAPE IS TRANSCRIBED, THE ROSTER IS A CLAIM, and they are separated on purpose.
# `content/browser/speech/tts_mac.mm:110-188` is a macOS Chrome's own list-building code, in this
# tree: every voice from `AVSpeechSynthesisVoice.speechVoices`, sorted by name ascending, with the
# system default moved to index 0, `lang` a BCP-47 tag, `native` true throughout. The mojo layer
# copies `name` into `voiceURI` too and marks index 0 default
# (`content/browser/speech/speech_synthesis_impl.cc:107`). All of that is reproduced rather than
# approximated. The roster -- Apple's single-name compact voices, one per language -- is the part Veil
# cannot verify without a Mac, and the table says so: the modern multi-language novelty voices (Eddy,
# Flo, Grandma, Grandpa, Reed, Rocko, Sandy, Shelley), whose duplicate names are the reason
# `tts_mac.mm` disambiguates at all, are deliberately not claimed. The error direction is a true set
# smaller than a maximal Mac's rather than an invented name, and the borrowed Mac T08 waits for
# settles it.
#
# ONE EMISSION PATH, NOT TWO. The counterpart patches the browser process *and* adds a renderer-side
# backfill in `blink::SpeechSynthesis`. This patch does the browser half only, and the omission is the
# argument rather than laziness:
#
# - Measured on this host: the browser path answers. Stock Chrome 151 reports 22 voices (3 local SAPI
#   plus 19 `Google ...` network voices) and this engine reports the 3 local ones -- ungoogled having
#   removed the Google TTS component -- so `OnSetVoiceList` arrives in both.
# - A renderer implementation of the same table would be a second source of one fact, which is the
#   rule 6.9 settled when it dropped the persona struct.
# - The renderer half's own stated purpose is the case where the browser never replies, and in that
#   case stock Chrome reports `[]` -- so does this engine, unchanged.
#
# And nothing is filled *synchronously*. `firstSyncCount: 0` measured on stock Chrome and on this
# engine: the first `getVoices()` on a fresh document returns `[]` and the list arrives with a
# `voiceschanged` event. A complete answer on the first call is an ordering tell independent of the
# values, and anti-bot vendors read it directly.
#
# A LINUX CLAIM REPORTS NO VOICES. `content/browser/speech/tts_linux.cc` enumerates through
# speech-dispatcher, which a desktop install frequently does not run, so an empty catalogue is an
# ordinary Linux reading. It is also the only honest one available: Veil has no Linux roster it can
# defend, and falling through would put Windows SAPI voices on a machine claiming Linux -- the same
# contradiction this patch removes, pointing the other way. `true` with an empty list is therefore a
# real answer in `ClaimedVoiceCatalogue`, not a failure to produce one.
#
# No switch is added, and no `--disable-spoofing` key either. The behaviour follows `--fingerprint`,
# `--fingerprint-platform` and the locale switches, all of which exist; a new opt-out key would be a
# value no Veil field can emit, which is the same lie as a switch no Chromium has. The flag dialect
# does not grow and none of the three mirrors moves except `coherence.mjs`, which gains `voices` in
# `DRIVEN_SURFACES` so its two rules score as defects from here on.

